    "title": "Why You Should Rethink PWAs and Consider WebViews for Cross-Platform Apps",
    "link": "https://medium.com/@thomasaugot/why-you-should-rethink-pwas-and-consider-webviews-for-cross-platform-apps-6c1a4a39d6ce",
    "pubDate": "2025-02-16T20:35:16.712Z",
    "content": "Building cross-platform mobile apps is always a challenge. When my team set out to develop an app using Next.js, we expected a Progressive Web App (PWA) to be the perfect solution. After all, PWAs promise offline support, mobile-first performance, and an app-like experience without the need for native development.\n\nBut reality didn’t match expectations. We ran into major roadblocks: SSR complexiti",
    "fullContent": "Building cross-platform mobile apps is always a challenge. When my team set out to develop an app using Next.js, we expected a Progressive Web App (PWA) to be the perfect solution. After all, PWAs promise offline support, mobile-first performance, and an app-like experience without the need for native development.\n\nBut reality didn’t match expectations. We ran into major roadblocks: SSR complexities, caching headaches, and frustrating browser restrictions. What seemed like a simple approach quickly turned into a time-consuming battle.\n\nThat’s when we discovered WebViews. Instead of forcing PWAs to work within a mobile browser’s limitations, we embedded our web app inside a native container, unlocking better performance and deeper integration with mobile features. In this article, I’ll walk you through our experience, the limitations of PWAs, and why WebViews turned out to be a game-changer for our project.\n\nThe Challenge with PWAs in Next.js\n\nFor those unfamiliar, Next.js is a React framework designed around server-side rendering (SSR), static site generation (SSG), and hybrid approaches. It’s fantastic for SEO, dynamic routing, and performance optimizations. But when it comes to building a PWA, it introduces some tricky challenges.\n\nWhy PWAs Struggle in Next.js\n\nAt this point, we started questioning whether a PWA was the best approach for our project. We needed an alternative that preserved the benefits of a web-based app without the restrictions of PWAs.\n\nThat’s when a colleague suggested we look into WebViews.\n\nWhat Exactly is a WebView?\n\nA WebView is a native container that renders web content inside a mobile app. Instead of running an app in the browser (like a PWA), a WebView loads your web app inside a native wrapper, allowing you to integrate mobile-specific features while maintaining a web-based architecture.\n\nKey Benefits of WebViews Over PWAs:\n\n✅ Full Access to Native Features - Unlike PWAs, WebViews can directly interact with mobile APIs, allowing better integration with camera, GPS, push notifications, sensors, and file storage.\n✅ Reliable Offline Support - Instead of relying on service workers, WebViews can preload and cache assets locally, ensuring a smoother offline experience.\n✅ Bypasses Browser Limitations - Since WebViews are part of a native app, they aren’t restricted by browser security policies, avoiding issues like Safari’s service worker limitations.\n✅ Easier Implementation - No need to configure service workers, caching strategies, or browser compatibility fixes, just load your web app inside a WebView, and you’re good to go.\n\nGet Thomas Augot’s stories in your inbox\n\nJoin Medium for free to get updates from this writer.\n\nWith these advantages in mind, we decided to shift our approach and implement WebViews for our app.\n\nHow to Implement a WebView in Your Mobile App\n\nImplementing a WebView is surprisingly simple. Without knowledge of Kotlin or Swift, a bit of Chat GPT and here’s how we did it for both iOS and Android.\n\nWebView in Swift (iOS)\n\nFor iOS apps, you can use WKWebView to embed a web page inside a native container:\n\nTo ensure offline functionality, you can preload assets inside the app bundle rather than depending on external network requests.\n\nWebView in Kotlin (Android)\n\nFor Android apps, WebView can be used similarly:\n\nAgain, for offline support, you can cache assets locally and serve them from within the app.\n\nChallenges and Considerations\n\nBefore jumping all in on WebViews, here are some things to keep in mind:\n\n🛑 Memory Consumption — WebViews run a mini browser inside your app, which can be memory-intensive. Optimize assets and scripts for performance.\n🛑 Platform-Specific Differences — WebViews behave slightly differently on iOS and Android. Some features that work on one platform may require tweaks on the other.\n🛑 App Store Guidelines — Apple and Google don’t like apps that are just web wrappers. You need to integrate some native functionality to get approved.\n🛑 Debugging Can Be Tricky — WebViews don’t always behave like a normal browser, so testing and debugging require extra effort.\n\nConclusion: WebView vs. PWA. Which One Should You Use?\n\nAfter struggling with PWAs and their limitations in Next.js, WebViews provided a cleaner, more flexible solution for our mobile app.\n\nWhen to Use WebViews:\n✔️ If you need full native access to mobile features (camera, GPS, etc.).\n✔️ If offline support is a priority and service worker caching is too complex.\n✔️ If you want to avoid browser restrictions like Safari’s PWA limitations.\n\nWhen PWAs Might Still Work:\n✔️ If your app is purely web-based and doesn’t need deep native integration.\n✔️ If you want a fully browser-driven experience without app store submissions.\n\nFor our use case, WebViews were the clear winner. They allowed us to build a cross-platform app using Next.js without the struggles of PWAs. If you’re facing similar challenges, I highly recommend trying WebViews . It might save you time, frustration, and unnecessary complexity.\n\nIf you liked this article, feel free to connect with me!",
    "image": "https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1200/1*tDgmmRH3dF02KzVFqXE4og.png",
    "categories": [
      "webview",
      "nextjs",
      "mobile-app-development",
      "cross-platform",
      "react"
    ],
    "readTime": 5
