    "content": "Today, I want to share a neat trick I recently discovered while working on a chat component in a React project. It’s all about ensuring that whenever new messages are added, the chat list automatically scrolls to the bottom, displaying the latest messages without making the user manually scroll down every time. Trust me, it’s a game-changer for user experience and I quite struggled to find a solut",
    "fullContent": "Today, I want to share a neat trick I recently discovered while working on a chat component in a React project. It’s all about ensuring that whenever new messages are added, the chat list automatically scrolls to the bottom, displaying the latest messages without making the user manually scroll down every time. Trust me, it’s a game-changer for user experience and I quite struggled to find a solution to it!\n\nImagine this: you’re building a chat feature for your app, and as messages flow in, you want the chat interface to stay focused on the latest conversation. However, as messages pile up, the list starts to overflow, requiring users to scroll down manually. Not very user-friendly…\n\nThe solution lies in leveraging React’s useEffect hook along with refs to manage the scrolling behavior dynamically. Here’s how we did it step-by-step:\n\nStep 1: An Example Code of a Chat Component\n\nFirstly, we set up a ChatThread component where the chat messages are displayed. Here’s a simplified version:\n\nStep 2: Understanding useRef and useEffect\n\nStep 3: Ensuring Smooth User Experience\n\nBy combining useRef for DOM manipulation and useEffect for managing side effects, we achieve a seamless scrolling experience for our chat users. The chat list stays focused on the latest messages without disrupting the user flow with manual scrolling.\n\nGet Thomas Augot’s stories in your inbox\n\nJoin Medium for free to get updates from this writer.\n\nI hope this guide helps you in your own projects! Feel free to adapt these concepts to suit your specific application needs. Happy coding!\n\nIf this article helped you, feel free to connect with me!",
