    "content": "Ever wanted to take your React website global and cater to a wider audience speaking different languages? Well, fret no more, because today we’ll dive into the wonderful world of internationalization (i18n) using the awesome react-i18next library.\n\nSetting up the stage: Installation and Initial Configuration\n\nFirst things first, let’s get react-i18next and its core dependency, i18next, installed i",
    "fullContent": "Ever wanted to take your React website global and cater to a wider audience speaking different languages? Well, fret no more, because today we’ll dive into the wonderful world of internationalization (i18n) using the awesome react-i18next library.\n\nSetting up the stage: Installation and Initial Configuration\n\nFirst things first, let’s get react-i18next and its core dependency, i18next, installed in your project using npm:\n\nNext, create an i18n.js file inside your project's root directory. This file will house the configuration for our i18n magic. If you prefer, you may also simply add this bunch of code to your app.js, depending on your preferences. I tend to go for a separate file to keep the code clean and organized. Here's a basic setup:\n\nThis code imports the necessary libraries, sets the default language (en in this case), and enables raw HTML interpolation within translations (useful for dynamic content) and also detects the browser’s language so that it will choose the language to use on its own.\n\nBuilding the Language Castle: Folders and Files\n\nNow, let’s create a folder structure to hold our translation files. I chose to call it “locales”, feel free to find a better one! Here’s a common approach:\n\nEach language file (e.g., en.json, fr.json) will contain key-value pairs representing the actual translations. Here's an example for fr.json:\n\nTranslating your Content: The Code that Makes it Happen\n\nNow comes the fun part: integrating translations into your React components! Here’s how:\n\nIn this example, the useTranslation hook returns a t function that you can use to access translations by their key. So, {t('welcome')} renders the translated text for the \"welcome\" key from the appropriate language file.\n\nWrapping Up: Multilingual Magic Awaits!\n\nWith these steps, you’ve successfully laid the foundation for a multilingual website using react-i18next. Remember to add translations for your entire website content and explore the library's advanced features like language detection and dynamic switching.\n\nGet Thomas Augot’s stories in your inbox\n\nJoin Medium for free to get updates from this writer.\n\nBy embracing i18n, you’ll open your website to a broader audience and create a truly inclusive user experience. So, go forth and conquer the world, one translation at a time!\n\nIn the meantime, you can visit my webpage at https://thomasaugot.com/ and connect with me on Medium or Linkedin https://www.linkedin.com/in/thomas-augot/ :)",
